/**************************************************************************************************
 * CNC3D "EYES" -- Command & Conquer rendered from N64 assets, driven by the live GPL brain.
 *
 * SDL2 + OpenGL 1.x fixed-function. It dlopens the Tiberian Dawn logic DLL, starts a real
 * mission, ticks it at the engine's native 15 Hz, and draws:
 *      - the terrain, one textured quad per cell, from the N64 tile banks
 *      - buildings / vehicles / terrain objects as N64 meshes, with their ROM textures
 *      - infantry as 2D billboarded sprites, because that is what the N64 did
 *
 * There is deliberately NO shader, NO VBO, NO GL 3.x anything: every draw is glBegin/glEnd
 * triangles with a modelview/projection matrix pair, because the eventual target is Glide on
 * a 3dfx Voodoo2, which has no programmable pipeline at all. Each glBegin block maps
 * one-to-one onto grDrawTriangle with a hand-built transform.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ASSETS
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Everything comes from a single baked pack (SCEN.pack, built by bake.py): textures already
 * decoded to RGBA and padded to power-of-two, meshes already flattened to a triangle soup
 * with per-vertex UV and RGBA, terrain already resolved to per-cell atlas UVs. The renderer
 * therefore contains no JSON parser, no PNG decoder and no OBJ parser -- which is also what
 * the Win98 build will want.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE CAMERA / WORLD TRANSFORM
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *   world.x = cellX, world.z = cellY, world.y = height. One engine cell == one world unit.
 *
 *   N64 model units are EXACTLY 1/1024 of a cell (2^10). Read straight off the ROM, not
 *   fitted: the flat ground-overlay display lists decode to axis-aligned quads centred on
 *   the model origin, and an overlay covers precisely one cell --
 *       slot 180 (road strip)      x = -512 .. +511      <- one cell, edge to edge
 *       slot 183 (road patch)      x = -512 .. +511,  z = -510 .. +512
 *       slots 151/152 (tiberium)   x,z = -515 .. +515    <- 1024 + a 3-unit bleed per side
 *   A single-scale least-squares fit of all 37 confirmed civilian meshes V01..V37 against
 *   the art bound the engine declares (BuildingTypeClass::Dimensions() = BSIZE footprint
 *   minus a fifth) independently lands on 1/1054, i.e. on the same number within the
 *   scatter of C&C's art-vs-footprint mismatch.
 *
 *   The old 1/512 was calibrated on V30/V34-36/V12, believed to be one cell across. V30 is
 *   BSIZE_21 not 1x1, and the N64 art for all four badly underfills its declared footprint,
 *   so that anchor was worth nothing. Everything was drawn exactly 2x oversized.
 *
 *   Those same quads also settle the model origin: they run -512..+512, i.e. N64 meshes are
 *   authored centred on their own origin. So an object must be drawn at the centre of its
 *   FOOTPRINT, not at the centre of its top-left cell. The brain now reports that centre
 *   directly (BuildingClass::Center_Coord, which adds CenterOffset[Class->Size]), so the
 *   renderer keeps no building-size table of its own.
 *
 *   TWO CAMERAS, and the C key flips between them live. See the Camera section for the
 *   arithmetic; the short version:
 *
 *     CAM_N64 (the real one, default)
 *         PERSPECTIVE. 50 degree VERTICAL field of view, yaw exactly ZERO, pitch
 *         44.691 .. 52.712 degrees linearly coupled to the camera distance, distance
 *         2400 .. 3800 leptons (256 leptons = 1 cell), default 3000, look-at target at
 *         y = -1 lepton. Recovered from the ROM, not guessed: see n64_camera.json.
 *         Because the yaw is zero the map draws as an AXIS-ALIGNED SQUARE GRID. Screen
 *         X is world X, screen down is world +Z. It is not a diamond and never was.
 *
 *     CAM_ORTHO (the renderer's old guess, kept only for comparison)
 *         Orthographic, yaw 45 deg about Y, pitch atan(0.5) = 26.565 deg about X: the
 *         classic PC 2:1 C&C diamond, where a cell projects exactly twice as wide as
 *         tall. Neither the 45 nor the 26.565 appears anywhere in the N64 ROM, and the
 *         ROM calls guOrtho zero times. Kept so the difference can be seen, not because
 *         it is right.
 *
 *   Three things follow from perspective that the orthographic path never had to face,
 *   and all three are handled rather than hand-waved:
 *     - a sprite's size on SCREEN now depends on its depth. The billboard keeps a fixed
 *       world size and the projection does the shrinking, so the pick rectangle can no
 *       longer be "world size times zoom": it projects the quad's real corners.
 *     - the visible ground is a TRAPEZOID, wider at the top of the screen. Terrain
 *       drawing and the camera clamp both work off the four unprojected screen corners.
 *     - zoom runs the other way. Ours counted pixels per cell (bigger = closer); the
 *       console counts distance (bigger = further). One zoom_step() owns the sense.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * DRAW ORDER (matters, and is Glide-reproducible)
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *   1 seabed under holes       depth write on   (BOTTOM.IMG; see README)
 *   2 water surface           alpha 170/255, depth test, NO depth write
 *   3 terrain triangles       alpha cut, depth write on -- draws OVER the water, which
 *                             is what gives the shoreline its art-texel edge
 *   4 mesh opaque triangles   depth write on
 *   5 mesh shadows            alpha blend, depth test, NO depth write. AFTER the
 *                             bodies: the cartridge puts each model's shadow faces LAST
 *                             in its own display list.
 *   6 mesh cutout triangles   alpha test 0.5, depth write on
 *   7 infantry sprites        alpha test, billboarded to the fixed camera
 *
 * USAGE
 *   cnc_eyes [--scen SCB01EA] [--pack FILE] [--shot out.png --ticks N] ...
 *   see --help
 **************************************************************************************************/

